      },
      "id": 5,
      "title": "🔵  Layer 4 — Database & Storage",
      "type": "row",
      "panels": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "unit": "s",
              "color": {
                "mode": "palette-classic"
              },
              "custom": {
                "lineWidth": 2,
                "fillOpacity": 10
              },
              "thresholds": {
                "mode": "absolute",
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "color": "green",
                    "value": null
                  },
                  {
                    "color": "yellow",
                    "value": 0.1
                  },
                  {
                    "color": "red",
                    "value": 0.5
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          },
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 8,
            "w": 12,
            "x": 0,
            "y": 116
          },
          "id": 50,
          "targets": [
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "prometheus"
              },
              "expr": "rate(db:query_duration_seconds_sum{job=\"$db_job\"}[$__rate_interval]) / rate(db:query_duration_seconds_count{job=\"$db_job\"}[$__rate_interval])",
              "legendFormat": "Query latency avg"
            },
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "prometheus"
              },
              "expr": "db:query_duration_seconds_max{job=\"$db_job\"}",
              "legendFormat": "Query latency max"
            }
          ],
          "title": "DB Query Latency (avg / max)",
          "type": "timeseries",
          "description": "**What it measures:** How long the database takes to execute queries — average (rate of total time / rate of total count) and the worst single query observed. Includes parse time, planning time, lock wait time, and execution time.\n\n**How collected:** exact aggregates from each vendor's own query-stats view (PostgreSQL `pg_stat_statements`, MySQL `performance_schema`, SQL Server `sys.dm_exec_query_stats`), via `db-exporter`. `max` is each vendor's own native per-query maximum, not derived — except **Oracle, which shows \"No data\" for max** since `v$sqlarea` has no per-call maximum column and a true one requires licensed Diagnostics Pack features (see `db-exporter/README.md`).\n\n**Why this replaced p95/p99:** none of these vendors' standard system views expose a true per-call latency distribution, only aggregates — the previous percentile panel was computed from an approximated/guessed histogram. Avg and max are both exact.\n\n**Thresholds:** Warn avg > 100ms · Critical avg > 500ms.\n\n**Common causes of latency spikes:** Missing index, stale statistics causing plan regression, lock contention, replication lag on read replicas, vacuum running on a large table.",
          "interval": "1m"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "unit": "percentunit",
              "color": {
                "mode": "thresholds"
              },
              "min": 0,
              "max": 1,
              "thresholds": {
                "mode": "absolute",
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "color": "green",
                    "value": null
                  },
                  {
                    "color": "yellow",
                    "value": 0.7
                  },
                  {
                    "color": "red",
                    "value": 0.9
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          },
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 8,
            "w": 6,
            "x": 12,
            "y": 124
          },
          "id": 51,
          "options": {
            "reduceOptions": {
              "calcs": [
                "lastNotNull"
              ]
            },
            "orientation": "auto"
          },
          "targets": [
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "prometheus"
              },
              "expr": "db:connections_active{job=\"$db_job\"} / (db:connections_max{job=\"$db_job\"} > 0)",
              "legendFormat": "{{database}}"
            }
          ],
          "title": "DB Active Connections %",
          "type": "gauge",
          "description": "**What it measures:** Active database connections as a percentage of the maximum allowed (`max_connections` in PostgreSQL).\n\n**Why it matters:** Approaching `max_connections` causes 'too many connections' errors that make the application completely unable to reach the database.\n\n**How collected:** `postgres_exporter` queries `pg_stat_activity` for active connection count and `pg_settings` for `max_connections`. The ratio is the saturation metric.\n\n**Thresholds:** Warn > 70% · Critical > 90%.\n\n**Best practice:** Use a connection pooler (PgBouncer) in front of PostgreSQL. This allows many application threads to share a small number of actual database connections, preventing exhaustion under load.\n\n**Note:** shows no data when `connections_max` is `0` (SQL Server/Postgres convention for \"unlimited/not explicitly capped\") -- a meaningless ratio otherwise, since dividing by zero produces `+Inf`. See the companion \"DB Active Connections (count)\" panel for a value that's always populated."
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "unit": "short",
              "color": {
                "mode": "thresholds"
              },
              "thresholds": {
                "mode": "absolute",
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "color": "green",
                    "value": null
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          },
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 8,
            "w": 6,
            "x": 6,
            "y": 124
          },
          "id": 56,
          "options": {
            "reduceOptions": {
              "calcs": [
                "lastNotNull"
              ],
              "fields": "",
              "values": false
            },
            "orientation": "auto",
            "textMode": "auto",
            "colorMode": "value",
            "graphMode": "area",
            "justifyMode": "auto"
          },
          "targets": [
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "prometheus"
              },
              "expr": "db:connections_active{job=\"$db_job\"}",
              "legendFormat": "{{database}}"
            }
          ],
          "title": "DB Active Connections (count)",
          "type": "stat",
          "description": "**What it measures:** Raw active connection count per database, independent of whether a `max_connections` cap is configured.\n\n**Why it exists:** The neighboring \"DB Active Connections %\" gauge shows nothing when `connections_max` is `0` -- SQL Server's (and some Postgres setups') convention for \"no explicit cap configured,\" which is the common case, not an edge case. This panel always has a number regardless.\n\n**Thresholds:** none set -- there's no universal \"too many\" for an absolute count; set one per-app if you have a known pool size limit at the application layer (e.g. a connection pooler)."
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "unit": "s",
              "color": {
                "mode": "palette-classic"
              },
              "custom": {
                "lineWidth": 2,
                "fillOpacity": 10
              },
              "thresholds": {
                "mode": "absolute",
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "color": "green",
                    "value": null
                  },
                  {
                    "color": "yellow",
                    "value": 10
                  },
                  {
                    "color": "red",
                    "value": 60
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          },
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 8,
            "w": 6,
            "x": 18,
            "y": 132
          },
          "id": 52,
          "targets": [
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "prometheus"
              },
              "expr": "db:replication_lag_seconds{job=\"$db_job\"}",
              "legendFormat": "{{instance}}"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Replication Lag",
          "type": "timeseries",
          "description": "**What it measures:** The delay between a write being committed on the primary database and that write being applied on replica databases, in seconds.\n\n**Why it matters:** Applications reading from replicas see stale data during lag. More critically, if the primary fails during high replication lag, the data written during that window is lost in a failover.\n\n**How collected:** `postgres_exporter` queries `pg_stat_replication` on the primary for bytes of WAL not yet replicated. MySQL: `mysqld_exporter` reads `Seconds_Behind_Master` from replica status.\n\n**Thresholds:** Warn > 10s · Critical > 60s. For financial data: Warn > 2s.\n\n**Common causes:** Network issues between primary and replica, replica under-resourced, very high write throughput on primary.",
          "interval": "1m"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "unit": "short",
              "color": {
                "mode": "palette-classic"
              },
              "custom": {
                "lineWidth": 2,
                "fillOpacity": 8
              }
            }
          },
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 8,
            "w": 9,
            "x": 6,
            "y": 140
          },
          "id": 54,
          "targets": [
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "prometheus"
              },
              "expr": "db:locks_total{job=\"$db_job\"} or vector(0)",
              "legendFormat": "{{database}} {{mode}}"
            },
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "prometheus"
              },
              "expr": "rate(db:deadlocks_total{job=\"$db_job\"}[$__rate_interval])",
              "legendFormat": "Deadlocks/s"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Lock Waits & Deadlocks",
          "type": "timeseries",
          "description": "**What it measures:** The number of database lock waits and deadlocks over time. Lock waits occur when a transaction needs a lock held by another transaction.\n\n**Why it matters:** Lock contention degrades throughput non-linearly — a few long-running transactions can block hundreds of others. Deadlocks cause transaction rollbacks and application errors.\n\n**How collected:** PostgreSQL: `pg_locks` view counts locks by type. MySQL: `Innodb_deadlocks` status variable via `mysqld_exporter`.\n\n**Thresholds:** Any increase in deadlocks should be investigated. Lock waits > 5 concurrent is Warn.\n\n**Common causes:** Long-running transactions, missing indexes causing full table scans that hold locks, application not committing transactions promptly.",
          "interval": "1m"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "prometheus"
          },
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "unit": "bytes",
              "color": {
                "mode": "palette-classic"
